※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4701

※ Taking an element out of a list needs somewhere to put what is left.
※
※ A list is where its elements are and how many there are, so a list with one
※ element fewer is a different pair of words.  Taking one out has to put those
※ words where whoever holds the list will read them -- a name, a field, an
※ element -- and a list that was just worked out has nowhere to be: the join
※ below makes one and nothing holds it.
※
※ The same rule `⎕iter` follows, and for the same reason: a walk may take
※ something out, so it holds the place and not the list.
※
※ Compare: **C++**, where `erase` is a member of the container and a temporary
※ container is a thing you may call it on -- to no effect anyone can see;
※ **Rust**, where `Vec::remove` on a temporary is refused by the borrow checker
※ for this reason.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let a: [u8] = [1u8, 2u8]
    let b: [u8] = [3u8]
    ※ the join is a value and not a place
    †(a ⧺ b)⟦0⟧
    0u6
